 for a meeting and there was a  piece of paper spitting out of his  dot-matrix printer for those of you not  familiar with the technology this was  the late 80s  on this page was a list and at the top  of the list were two letters a a and  beside it was the name of his wife and  his lawyer and I realized I was looking  at his Christmas list and a voice in me  said Billy stop reading because you  don't want to know where you are on this  list you don't want to know what letter  you are but several weeks later a  Christmas gift arrived from this  producers office and it was this  beautiful silver tray with a gingerbread  house on it that was inlaid with candies  and lights was fantastic and I thought  to myself well I'm not a a but I'm a and  I'm only 25 that's pretty good during  the course of the next year both movies  that I was writing for him went in to  turn around and as Christmas time rolled  around I found myself thinking I don't  think there's another silver tray coming  for me this year but soon a messenger  from his office came and presented me  with this great gift basket and inside  the gift basket was a bottle of wine and  salami and some cheese and I thought to  myself you know what I'm not a but I'm  still B and I'm only 26 that's not so  bad  and I were later I got a call from a  panic desist in who work for this  producer said did you get a gift from us  he said yes I did so don't open it I  said why not he said we've got your gift  mixed up with somebody else I said well  it's okay I mean he got mine I got his  it's really fine I said don't open it  someone's coming over right now he hung  up still palpitating and ten minutes  later I hear a screech of tires outside  my apartment and the frantic steps of  this panicked assistant as he pounds his  way up the stairs to my door where he  pounded and I answered and there he is  ashen-faced he says you have it and I  give him the gift basket and he handed  me a tin of yogurt-covered pretzels  when people ask me what it's like to be  a young screenwriter in Hollywood that's  the story that I tell them in other  words  nickels fellows I am now officially  welcoming you into the fraternity of  screenwriters which means it's my job  both to congratulate you and to warn you  about what's out there ten years ago or  even five this warning would have been  simple back then when screenwriters get  to got together to [ __ ] which was often  it was usually about directors for being  bullies or for trying to steal a shared  screenplay by credit or cutting  necessary dialogue or just playing  botching things but that's not the case  anymore the sands under all of us have  shifted now and today directors even the  arrogant ones are voting scared like  everybody else  today the warning is actually about the  business itself and the almost  pathological timidity of the  institutions that control it when I  started writing there were still a few  Mavericks out there a few gunslingers  who were running studios these were  people who went with their guts and  would make a movie just because they  believed in it but that's not the  process anymore today before a studio  chair can greenlight a movie that movie  must also be blessed by the head of  marketing and the head of foreign sales  and the head of home video it must be  subjected to a process called running  the numbers which means that that movies  cost or downside is compared as compared  against its potential value because of  its cast and because of what might do in  foreign markets of course this process  takes into account every variable except  the variable which actually matters the  one that can't possibly be gauged by any  sort of calculus which is whether or not  the movie is going to be any good and  yet the process continues there are  challenges there are other challenges  too ones born in progress itself it's  axiomatic now that the technology of  moviemaking has never been greater than  it is today  but I don't think any of us can honestly  say that movies themselves have never  been greater and these things are not  unrelated suppose that we were going to  shoot a scene of this very ceremony in  this very room we can now if we want to  add elements to the  scene to make it more visually arresting  we could light the ceiling on fire by  using CGI we could enhance the  experience by projecting it in 3d does  any of this have anything to do with  story or character no but it would look  great in the trailer that kind of  thinking the idea that dazzling visuals  are enough has led to a certain kind of  moviemaking laziness that has not been  good for anybody  worse it's made it tougher and tougher  to dazzle the audience because they know  out there now that we're making movies  with software instead of cameras and  when any image is possible no image is  all that impressive anymore we're  storytellers which means we have to do  better sometimes I think we have to  rescue the business from the very people  who own it the good news is we can  inside every one of you Nichols fellows  is the flame that has always lived away  in this industry which is originality  that one spark of an idea that one  archetypal character or story or truth  or world that no one's ever captured  before you remember the movie wall-e the  brilliant Pixar film he's in a dangerous  world he's one of thousands who were  supposed to clean it up but there's  something special about wall-e he finds  this little tiny sprig which one which  might one day become a plant and he  guards it and saves it and preserves it  on the chance that it might someday turn  into something beautiful  well Hollywood is that dangerous world  and you are wall-e  and your idea is that plant and you have  to protect it if you do it might change  the world  that's your charge now greatness you  have to pursue it every single day in  everything that you do in your work  ethic in the way that you conduct  yourself and who you choose to do  business with and whether or not you  learn from them you are now a part of  the most collaborative medium anywhere  in the world and much of your success in  it is going to be determined by how well  you listen I was very fortunate in that  regard as the child of divorced parents  I learned early how to take two warring  factions and make them both feel that  they'd been heard this works fantastic  in meetings  you're gonna get a lot of notes in your  career and it'll be your instinct to  resent them but I'm telling you now to  ignore this instinct the fact is that  most of the notes that you get will be  smart and thoughtful and all of them  even the bad ones will teach you  something about where you fail to  articulate something as clearly as you  had as you had it in your head or where  something just didn't make it on the  page where something just didn't land  you will make your living largely not by  writing but by rewriting and the grace  and efficiency with which you do that  will define you we will mostly define  you more than anything else is what  projects you say yes to and what project  you say no to here's a rule of thumb  about that if someone offers you a job  on a project that has real heat a star  attached to it or some wonderful  pedigree and you find that you're not  waking you're not waking up every  morning thinking about it if it's not  living inside of you trying to claw its  way out then don't write it remember  that you got here by believing in  original thinking never forget that and  always remember that if you are inspired  we will be - the best advice I ever  heard about writing came from Paddy  Chayefsky he of the aforementioned  Network and the hospital he also wrote  Marty that's three Oscars Chayefsky  advice to writers was simple don't think  of it as art think of it as work because  when a writer is stuck and he or she  calls it another writer for help that  second writer doesn't say what's the art  problem that second writer says what's  not working and they get under the hood  and they fix it together that's most of  what you'll do in your career work  problem solving approach it in that way  and then at the end of every day you'll  at least be able to say I did my job  today and if you're an artist it'll come  out as art anyway the truth is there are  millions of variables that will  profoundly affect your career variables  over which you have zero control you  have no control about over the state of  the economy or over the health of our  industry  you have no say and whether or not the  DVD business flatlines or whether or not  the market is suddenly ripe for  thrillers or superhero movies or  god help us romantic comedies the only  variable you are at you will ever have  any control over is your willingness to  work hard so maximize that variable I  take my son to his bus stop every  morning at 7:30 I'm at my desk working  by 8:00 somebody feeds me at 1:00 and  I'm back at my desk by 1:30 working  until 6:00 I don't surf the web I don't  gamble online I don't go to the local  Starbucks for two hours I don't try to  seek out old girlfriends on Facebook I  don't do anything that requires time I  just work I do this because there's a  mountain top that I'm trying to get to  and I'm nowhere near it I can see it  from here at the accomplishments of  those writers that I mentioned earlier  that's the holy grail for me I want to  leave a legacy like they did I want to  spend a lot of time in tuxedos the next  time the da fi does a list of the  hundred greatest movies of all time I  want my name on board on one of them at  least  and I want someday in 2032 for someone  standing right here making this speech  to cite one of my movies with the same  kind of reverence that I have for those  movies of the 70s luck will not get me  there although I will need plenty of it  the only thing that will get me there is  my willingness to write that fifteenth  raft or proofread it one more time or be  open to notes from someone who's smarter  than I am it may be that I'll be getting  notes from one of you Nichols fellows  one day and I trust that they'll be good  ones I'll leave you with one last  thought as you surge forward don't  forget to look back at the writers guild  building on 3rd and Fairfax there's a  library and in that library you can read  any screenplay that you'd ever want to  read visit that library it's your  history up on those shelves the bar that  was set for you  by the writers that have preceded you  their triumphs their struggles they  belong to you now cherish them learn  from them let them challenge you to keep  getting better it's true there will be a  tin of yogurt-covered pretzels or two  thrown your way  but if you keep trying and if you keep  fighting it might one day be replaced on  yourself by an Oscar I wish you nothing  but the best and I thank you very much
 

